Tenko Linux 6 (Workstation) installer
======================================

tenko-installer is a Python 3 / ncurses installer for Tenko Linux
6 (Workstation).  It is UEFI-only and GPT-only.

The default path is a guided install: choose one disk, erase it or use its
free space, pick ext4 or btrfs, and the installer creates a working desktop
with a swapfile.  The advanced path is a multi-disk layout editor: on each
selected disk you can delete partitions, create new ones, and assign mount
points to new or existing partitions.  Existing entries are preserved unless
you mark them for deletion.

The application and its tests use the Python standard library only.  The
live image needs the packages listed by the tenko-live meta-package.

Running
-------

  tenko-installer                 interactive ncurses installer
  tenko-installer --plain         line-oriented serial fallback
  tenko-installer --answer FILE   non-interactive install
  tenko-installer --dry-run --answer FILE
                                     plan and log, execute no mutations
  tenko-installer --plan-json --answer FILE
                                     print the validated layout and exit
  tenko-installer --simulate      interactive UI with synthetic disks;
                                     forced dry-run, no root/live media
  tenko-installer --simulate-scenario mixed
                                     synthetic disks with an existing ESP
                                     and data partition
  tenko-installer --no-self-update  never check the repository for a newer
                                     installer and restart

`--simulate` is for UI development: it runs the real curses flow, planner
and install pipeline against in-memory disks.  No block device is opened or
modified; all mutating commands go to `plan.sh` in the simulation directory
(default `/tmp/tenko-installer-sim`, override with `TENKO_SIM_DIR`).
From a checkout, use:

  scripts/run_installer_sim.sh
  scripts/run_installer_sim.sh --simulate-scenario mixed

The kernel command line can supply:

  tenko.answer=/path/to/answer.json
  tenko.auto=1                    same as --non-interactive
  tenko.console=serial            same as --plain
  tenko.dry_run=1                 same as --dry-run

Guided install
--------------

After the preflight screen the installer offers two paths:

  Install Tenko Linux 6 (Workstation) (recommended)
  Advanced installation

If the package repository is unreachable, the installer shows a network
screen instead of exiting: it lists the NetworkManager devices, opens
`nmtui-connect` (or `nmtui`) when asked, and retries the repository check.
The same retry is offered in `--plain`/serial mode; an `--answer` install
reports the nmtui hint and keeps the longer automatic retry.  nmtui runs
with a UTF-8 locale inside a kmscon VT, so non-ASCII SSIDs are not dropped
and render with the CJK console font stack.

Once the repository is reachable, the curses flow checks whether it contains
a newer tenko-installer.  If it does, the installer offers to upgrade the
running copy in the live root's writable overlay and restart itself with the
same arguments.  An older ISO can therefore pick up current installer fixes
without a media rebuild.  The check is skipped in `--simulate`, `--dry-run`,
non-interactive and installed-system runs, and a failed check or update
simply leaves the current installer running.

The guided path asks for:

  1. the target disk (one disk; other disks are not touched)
  2. erase the disk or use its existing free space
  3. ext4 or btrfs, and swapfile / swap partition / no swap
  4. the account, hostname, timezone/locale/keymap and package set

It then shows a review page with every deletion and creation and installs
after one confirmation.  A guided erase marks every existing partition on
the selected disk for deletion; the review page lists them.  "Use the free
space" never deletes an existing partition and adopts an existing ESP
without formatting it.

The advanced path keeps the full multi-disk editor.  Selecting a partition
row now opens that partition's actions directly (assign/edit, delete, keep);
there is no need to type a partition number.  Sizes in the new-partition
form are entered in GiB (a bare number is GiB; `512M`, `20G` and `20GiB` are
also accepted).

Answer file
-----------

The file is JSON.  Version 2 describes one layout with a list of disks;
version 3 is the guided form (mode + disk).  Each disk has a path and an
optional list of partitions.  A partition is either newly created (role +
fstype + mountpoint + size_mib) or adopts an existing entry
(existing_number, format false).  A minimal single-disk example is:

  {
    "version": 2,
    "layout": {
      "disks": [
        {
          "path": "/dev/vda",
          "serial": "QM00001",
          "size_bytes": 21474836480,
          "delete_partitions": [],
          "partitions": [
            {"role": "esp", "fstype": "vfat",
             "mountpoint": "/boot", "size_mib": 1024},
            {"role": "swap", "fstype": "swap", "size_mib": 2048},
            {"role": "root", "fstype": "ext4", "mountpoint": "/"}
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "package_set": "desktop",
    "hostname": "tenko",
    "timezone": "UTC",
    "locale": "C.UTF-8",
    "keymap": "us",
    "admin": {"username": "admin", "password": "change-me"},
    "confirm": {"disk": "/dev/vda", "phrase": "ERASE"}
  }

Optional top-level keys are `swap_mode` (`partition`, `swapfile` or `none`,
default `partition`), `swap_mib` (default 2048), `timezone` (default `UTC`),
`locale` (default `C.UTF-8`) and `keymap` (default `us`).  A locale other
than C, POSIX or C.UTF-8 adds `glibc-locales-extra` to the target.  A
`swapfile` layout must not contain a swap partition:

  "swap_mode": "swapfile",
  "swap_mib": 2048

``size_mib`` may be omitted to fill the remaining free region.  A partition
that is mounted at an arbitrary absolute path has role ``data``; this is
how /home is put on a second disk:

  {
    "version": 2,
    "layout": {
      "disks": [
        {
          "path": "/dev/vda",
          "partitions": [
            {"role": "esp", "fstype": "vfat",
             "mountpoint": "/boot", "size_mib": 1024},
            {"role": "swap", "fstype": "swap", "size_mib": 2048},
            {"role": "root", "fstype": "btrfs", "mountpoint": "/"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "path": "/dev/vdb",
          "partitions": [
            {"role": "data", "fstype": "ext4", "mountpoint": "/home"}
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    ...
  }

To keep an existing partition without formatting it, give its
``existing_number`` and set ``format`` to false.  The layout must contain
exactly one root (``/``) and one ESP (``/boot``); every mount point must be
unique.  Filesystems are ext4, btrfs, vfat and swap.

The answer file contains a plaintext test password.  Keep it mode 0600 and
remove it from the live media after use.  The installer never writes the
password into a log or into plan.sh.

Version 3 is the guided form; the planner creates the layout from the
selected disk:
  {
    "version": 3,
    "mode": "erase-disk",
    "disk": "/dev/vda",
    "filesystem": "ext4",
    "swap": "swapfile",
    "package_set": "desktop",
    "hostname": "tenko",
    "timezone": "UTC",
    "locale": "C.UTF-8",
    "keymap": "us",
    "admin": {"username": "admin", "password": "change-me"},
    "confirm": {"phrase": "ERASE"}
  }

``mode`` is ``erase-disk`` or ``free-space``; ``swap`` is ``swapfile``,
``partition`` or ``none``.  ``encrypt`` is accepted only as false until LUKS
support lands.  Version 2 layouts remain supported.

Layout policy
-------------

  * GPT only.  MBR disks are refused with an explanation.
  * Every selected disk is edited independently.  New partitions are
    placed in free regions; the editor shows exactly which entries will be
    deleted and which will be preserved.
  * An existing EFI System Partition can be adopted as /boot, with a warning
    when it is too small for systemd-boot plus a kernel and initramfs.
    Otherwise a 1 GiB FAT32 ESP is created.
  * Root is ext4 or btrfs, at least 8 GiB when newly created, and fills the
    remaining region unless a size is given.
  * Swap is optional.  The guided install defaults to a 2 GiB swapfile on
    the root filesystem; the advanced default is also a swapfile.  A swap
    partition can still be requested for hibernation.
  * Data partitions can use ext4, btrfs or vfat and may be mounted at any
    absolute path except the system-owned /boot, /proc, /sys, /dev, /run
    and /tmp trees.  /home on a second disk is the common case.
  * The GPT tail gap ends at the last usable LBA, never at the device end;
    the backup GPT is never included.

Safety features
---------------

  * The live medium is detected from /run/initramfs/livedev, the
    /run/initramfs/live mount source and the TENKO label, and is never
    selectable.  Failure to identify it is a hard failure.
  * Mounted, swapped, read-only, holder-bearing (LVM/RAID/dm) and
    whole-disk-filesystem devices are refused with the specific reason.
  * The complete table is snapshotted (text and binary sectors) before any
    change.  Addition/modification commands are emitted by the pure planner.
  * After writing, every untouched partition is compared by number, start,
    size, type, partition UUID, name and attributes.  A mismatch restores
    the saved table automatically before the first mkfs.
  * A plan that deletes existing partitions, or a custom layout, requires
    typing the root disk path and the word ERASE.  A guided plan that only
    creates partitions uses the review screen and a single confirmation.
  * --dry-run executes only read-only probes and writes plan.sh; no
    mutating command and no target file write is performed.

Install flow
------------

  1. for every changed disk: snapshot, delete selected entries, write the
     full replacement GPT, re-read, verify preservation (rollback on
     mismatch)
  2. mkfs.fat, mke2fs/ext4, mkfs.btrfs or mkswap for every partition that
     needs a filesystem; blkid verifies the type and UUID
  3. mount root, the ESP at /boot, every data partition at its mount point,
     create and enable a swapfile when selected, enable swap partitions,
     bind /dev /proc /sys /run, efivarfs
  4. dnf --installroot: tenko-basefs in its own first transaction
  5. dnf --installroot: tenko-base + xfce-desktop + kernel/modules +
     dracut + efibootmgr + dosfstools + btrfs-progs + sudo + dnf
  6. fstab by UUID for every mount and swap area, hostname, admin user in
     wheel and kvm, root locked, first-boot marker, systemd presets and
     graphical.target
  7. depmod + dracut --force --no-hostonly
  8. chroot bootctl --esp-path=/boot --no-variables install; loader.conf and
     loader/entries/tenko-<kver>.conf by UUID; EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI
     fallback; best-effort efibootmgr NVRAM entry; /etc/kernel/cmdline for
     future kernel packages
  9. copy install.jsonl/install.log to the target and the ESP, unmount

Logs
----

  /run/tenko/install.jsonl   JSON Lines, one event/command per line
  /run/tenko/install.log     human-readable mirror
  /run/tenko/plan.json       validated layout
  /run/tenko/plan.sh         dry-run command script
  /run/tenko/backup/         sfdisk text dump + binary sector backup

On success the logs are copied to /var/log/tenko-install/ on the target
and to the root of the ESP (tenko-install.jsonl/.log).  On failure the
tail is copied to the ESP as tenko-install-failure.jsonl/.log so it
survives the reboot.  Passwords are redacted everywhere.

Tests
-----

From the repository root, without root and without touching a disk:

  python3 -m unittest discover -s package_specs/tenko-installer/tests -v

The planner tests use the JSON fixtures in tests/fixtures/.
